 ### Zucker-Hummel Operator
-#### definition
-- [S4, unofficial] Advanced gradient operators that use 54 voxels to produce softer and clearer gradients compared to the Central-Difference operator.
-#### step
-- [S4, unofficial] 1. Apply operator masks in X, Y, and Z planes at positions one voxel before, at, and one voxel after the sampling point. 2. Multiply voxel values by mask coefficients. 3. Sum the results for each plane to get gradient components.
-#### comparison
-- [S4, unofficial] Unlike the Central-Difference operator which uses 6 voxels, Zucker-Hummel and Sobel 3D use 54 voxels, providing a better description of local surface structure.
-- [S4, unofficial] More complex calculation than Central-Difference, requiring 54 sampling points, but offers significantly improved image quality.
+- **Definition**: Advanced gradient operators that use 54 voxels to produce softer and clearer gradients compared to the Central-Difference operator [S4, unofficial].
+- **Step**: 1. Apply operator masks in X, Y, and Z planes at positions one voxel before, at, and one voxel after the sampling point. 2. Multiply voxel values by mask coefficients. 3. Sum the results for each plane to get gradient components [S4, unofficial].
+- **Comparison**: Unlike the Central-Difference operator which uses 6 voxels, Zucker-Hummel and Sobel 3D use 54 voxels, providing a better description of local surface structure [S4, unofficial]. More complex calculation than Central-Difference, requiring 54 sampling points, but offers significantly improved image quality [S4, unofficial].
 
 ### Hessian-Vector Product via Central Differences
-#### definition
-- [S5] An approximation of the Hessian-vector product $H(\theta)v$ that avoids computing the full Hessian matrix.
-- [S5] Estimation of the Hessian-vector product $D_{HVP} = \nabla^2 v = \partial^2 / \partial\tau_i \partial\tau \cdot \partial/\partial\tau$, which is the directional gradient of the gradient, resulting in an n-dimensional vector.
-#### step
-- [S5] Evaluate the first-order gradient estimator at positions shifted from the current solution along the direction $v$ by $\pm \epsilon$.
-#### formula
-- [S5] Approximation using central differences: $H(\theta)v \approx \lim_{\epsilon \to 0} \frac{g(\theta + \epsilon v) - g(\theta - \epsilon v)}{2\epsilon}$.
-- [S5] The smooth HVP kernel is estimated using directional central differences of the gradient estimator: $D_{HVP} \kappa(\tau) = \frac{D_G \kappa(\tau - \epsilon v) - D_G \kappa(\tau + \epsilon v)}{2\epsilon}$.
-#### complexity
-- [S5] Time complexity: $O(n)$ function evaluations without IS, $O(n)$ with IS, $O(1)$ with Aggregate IS. Space complexity for Aggregate IS is $O(n)$.
+- **Definition**: An approximation of the Hessian-vector product $H(\theta)\mathbf{v}$ that avoids computing the full Hessian matrix [S5]. It estimates the directional gradient of the gradient, resulting in an n-dimensional vector [S5].
+- **Step**: Evaluate the first-order gradient estimator at positions shifted from the current solution along the direction $\mathbf{v}$ by $\pm \epsilon$ [S5].
+- **Formula**: Approximation using central differences: $H(\theta)\mathbf{v} \approx \lim_{\epsilon \to 0} \frac{\mathbf{g}(\theta + \epsilon \mathbf{v}) - \mathbf{g}(\theta - \epsilon \mathbf{v})}{2\epsilon}$ [S5]. The smooth HVP kernel is estimated using directional central differences of the gradient estimator: $D_{HVP} \kappa(\tau) = \frac{D_G \kappa(\tau - \epsilon \mathbf{v}) - D_G \kappa(\tau + \epsilon \mathbf{v})}{2\epsilon}$ [S5].
+- **Complexity**: Time complexity: $O(n)$ function evaluations without IS, $O(n)$ with IS, $O(1)$ with Aggregate IS. Space complexity for Aggregate IS is $O(n)$ [S5].
 
 ## Background
-Finite difference methods are foundational in numerical analysis and computer graphics. In volume rendering, they were adopted early to provide surface normals for shading when explicit geometry was unavailable. The distinction between simple central differences and more complex operators like Sobel or Zucker-Hummel emerged from the need to balance noise robustness against computational cost. The Zucker-Hummel operator, for instance, was developed to provide "softer and clearer" gradients by incorporating more spatial context (54 voxels vs 6) [S4, unofficial]. In differentiable rendering, finite differences are often used as a baseline for gradient estimation, with higher-order schemes like WENO5 introduced to reduce truncation error in smooth regions [S3]. The use of finite differences for Jacobian and Hessian estimation in deformation fields and optimization tasks (like Hessian-vector products) extends their utility beyond simple shading into physics simulation and inverse graphics [S1, S5].
+Finite difference methods are a cornerstone of numerical analysis for approximating derivatives of functions defined on discrete grids. In the context of volume rendering, they were adopted to provide surface normals for Phong shading and other lighting models when the volume data was represented as a dense grid of scalar values without explicit surface extraction [S1]. The central difference scheme, being second-order accurate and unbiased, became the standard default due to its balance of accuracy and simplicity [S1]. Variants like forward/backward differences are used when boundary conditions or directional bias are acceptable, while higher-order schemes (like 4th-order or WENO) are employed for high-fidelity applications such as SDF rendering or fluid simulation [S3][S4, unofficial]. The method is widely used in scientific visualization, medical imaging, and physics-based rendering pipelines.
 
